          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, particularly hybrid retrieval combining sparse and dense search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\n  - pattern/Hybrid-Retrieval\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Updated with substantive description of hybrid retrieval approach; single primary source with clear justification.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\n**Hybrid retrieval** combines sparse lexical search (such as BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets\\u2014often using reciprocal rank fusion\\u2014to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Strengths\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix:\\n- **Rare identifiers** (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- **Natural language intent**\\n\\nThis makes it particularly effective for real-world information needs that cannot be satisfied by exact matching or semantic similarity alone.\\n\\n### Production Use\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) systems, reflecting its practical superiority over single-method approaches.\\n\\n## Merging Strategies\\n\\n**Reciprocal rank fusion** is a robust approach for combining results from multiple retrieval methods, providing a principled way to balance contributions from each ranking signal.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Ingest source (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-10) \\u2014 describes hybrid retrieval combining sparse and dense search methods.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
